 {:seon.task/name "a-connections-node-cache-is-kept-across-fork"
  :seon.task/plan "On a jolt/<topic> branch of our Datahike fork: a function of datahike.connections that, in a forked child, forgets every connection (their handles are the parent's) and keeps each node cache, so the child's first connect to that store adopts it (reserve-connection-opening! already shares a cache by node store and threshold). seon.db/after-fork! does this today by rewriting the registry itself: each entry becomes {:count 0 :node-store-key :threshold :node-cache} under [:seon.db/inherited node-store-key threshold] (seon.db-test/a-fork-keeps-its-parents-node-caches-and-no-connection), which leans on the registry's keys. With the library's own function, after-fork! calls it and the odd entries go. Measured with the registry rewrite: a fresh agent's first observe 50M ticks to 21.6M, its first render no dearer than a later one (task seon.agent/an-agents-first-read-costs-what-a-later-one-does). Proof: the same test, calling the library function."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-fork-keeps-its-parents-node-caches-and-no-connection}}]
